            <section>
               <title value="Hospital course note"/>
               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, thank you for referring your 57-year-old male patient, admitted on 22/06/2007 with an acute exacerbation of known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Physical examination confirmed bilateral expiratory wheezing. Chest X-ray showed pulmonary hyperinflation without evidence of pneumonia or pneumothorax. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed mild hypoxaemia with hypercapnia. Blood tests showed moderate inflammatory markers. The patient was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and close monitoring of vital signs and oxygen saturation. He responded well to treatment with significant symptomatic improvement. Pulmonary rehabilitation advice and inhaler technique education were provided prior to discharge. We recommend continuation of optimised inhaler therapy and close outpatient follow-up. Please do not hesitate to contact us should further information be required. Yours sincerely.</div>
               </text>
